 "notes": "Device paper: the 'material' spine is thin (sol-gel PZT -> patterned ribbon network -> conformal device); the argument's weight is in the device transfer function (PRP m11, object=CMS on substrate), its validation against DMA on PDMS (PRF/qualification m17), and three application PRFs (skin, lesions, organs). Tissue moduli are properties of the test object, not of the made material; I routed them through PRF/service_capability of the device rather than PRP of skin. F3/F4/F6 moduli are CMS outputs: F3 is split into voltage (measured) -> modulus (derived) because the dual axis makes the conversion explicit (proposed op convert_via_calibration, calibration = F2b linear V-E relation); F4/F6 moduli kept as measured instrument outputs. Audit m32 (text-silent): skin moduli (MPa) are outside the PDMS calibration range (<=1.76 MPa), so the calibration that turns voltage into modulus is extrapolated ~2-4x; this bears on every skin PRF. Audit m27 (text-silent): the dehydration explanation for the DMA-CMS gap is weak by the paper's own F6g numbers, leaving a possible systematic CMS underestimate. F3d (volume-rendered skin, greyscale colour/opacity) is not discussed in the packet text; omitted. Lost: rotatable mapping system (F5), biocompatibility, glycerin/urea results.",
 "migrated_from": "r03/graphs/Nature_Materials__10.1038_nmat4289.json"
}